    "text": "# Merging Overlapping Closed Integer Intervals\n\n```python\ndef merge_intervals(intervals):\n    \"\"\"\n    Merge overlapping closed integer intervals.\n\n    Args:\n        intervals: iterable of (start, end) pairs, inclusive on both ends.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new sorted list of merged (start, end) tuples.\n        The input is not mutated.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not intervals:\n        return []\n\n    # Copy + sort so we never touch the caller's data.\n    # Sort by start, then by end (tie-break keeps output deterministic).\n    ordered = sorted((tuple(iv) for iv in intervals), key=lambda iv: (iv[0], iv[1]))\n\n    merged = [ordered[0]]\n    for start, end in ordered[1:]:\n        last_start, last_end = merged[-1]\n        # Closed intervals overlap or touch when start <= last_end + 1.\n        # (Use `start <= last_end` instead if you want strict overlap only.)\n        if start <= last_end + 1:\n            if end > last_end:\n                merged[-1] = (last_start, end)\n        else:\n            merged.append((start, end))\n\n    return merged\n```\n\n## The Invariant\n\nAt the top of each loop iteration, `merged` satisfies:\n\n1. **Sorted & disjoint** \u2014 every interval in `merged` has a strictly smaller start than the next, and consecutive intervals do not overlap or touch (`merged[i].end + 1 < merged[i+1].start`).\n2.",
